'''Araucaria''' ({{Lang-ku|Pucará}}; meaning "fortified place"; {{Langx|mao|Arawkàrya}}) is a {{W|confederation|confederated}} [[Communities of Balisca|community]] within [[Balisca]]. The community takes its name from the [[w:Araucaria|eponymous trees]] that are abundant across it's territory. With an area of {{convert|621857.83|km2|0|abbr=on}}, it is the x-largest territory in Balisca. To the north it is bordered by [[Hesperia]] and the [[Castejón Metropolis]], is bounded by the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] to the east, [[Asikyira]] to the south, and [[Pindorama]] to the west. It is bisected by the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]]. With over thirty-six million inhabitants in 2026, it is the third most populous of the Baliscan confederacy. Araucaria is home to Balisca’s fifth largest metropolitan area, [[Axarquía]], which also serves as the ''de facto'' Araucarian capital. Other large cities in the region include [[Yerba Buena]], [[León]], [[Carabassa]], [[Porciúncula]], and [[Jatiel]]. It has ten regions; [[Arapytúcaá]], [[Juyú]], [[Brasil]], [[Karuãguasu]], [[Cabu Traïdor]], [[Cabu Verd]], [[Jaguarundi]], [[Tukupi]], [[Luzamontaña]] and [[tba]].

The area that today corresponds to the community's territory was already inhabited by various [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous peoples]] from approximately 12,000 BC. The [[Aruanãyára Empire]], the largest and most complex of the pre-colonial Abyalan societies exercised control over much of what is now Araucarian territory In the early 16th century, the coast of the region was visited by [[Kingdom of Burawa|Burawi]] explorers and navigators. [[Burawi colonization of Abyala|Burawi colonization]] of the land began with the [[Burawi conquest of the Aruanãyára|waging of war against the Aruanãyára]]. Despite the capture of [[Takûakesétyba]] by the Burawi, fierce Indigenous resistance continued, and colonial control and settlement remained minimal. This lack of colonial control led the territory to become known as [[La Frontera|''La Frontera'']], with the gap being filled by [[Maroons|Indigenous refugees, rebelling slaves, convicts and bandits]] often from Volisanian colonies. Due to its relatively brutal reputation, the territory  was prohibitively known to authorities as [[Maroon Confederacy|'''''Satanazes''''']] ("isle of devils"), and it quickly became a haven for runaways, [[Maroon pirates|pirates]], and those [[Maroon Wars|actively fighting to end slavery]]. The [[Baliscan Civil War]] and the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] (which ''{{W|de jure}}'' recognized Araucaria as a state of former slaves and their descendants) brought significant demographic changes to the region.

Araucaria is an {{W|advanced economy|advanced}} {{W|Participatory economics|participatory economy}} with a long history of {{W|economic democracy}}; if considered a sovereign state, it would be the ?th largest economy in the world. Major sectors of the Araucarian economy include transportation, education, information technology and research, government services, construction, and tourism. 

Araucaria is home to one of the largest populations of people of Kaftian descent in the [[Algarves]], with a plurality of its inhabitants being descendants of [[Aethiopian slave trade|Kaftian slaves brought to Balisca]] in the colonial era. Additionally, it is home to significant numbers of [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous people]] (including those who inhabited the land prior to the Illypnian colonization and those displaced afterwards) mostly of the [[Kurupiranga]] [[W:Ethnolinguistic group|ethnolinguistic group]], and [[Mozbayán|Mozbayáns]]. 

==History==
