===Indigenous history===
{{Main|Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Matarrañyan civilizations|Aruanãyára Empire}}

====Kafto-Illypnian arrival and Maroon conflict====
{{Main|New Burawa|Province of Arakwaya}}
[[File:Fort Samara.jpg|left|thumb|The [[Fort Samara|fortifications of Adka Samara]] in [[Cusúsamara]] plantation marked the beginning of the [[Maroon Wars]]]]
In the early 16th century, the east coast of Abyala was visited by Burawi explorers and navigators, and the area was granted to the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] under the [[Abyala Charter]]. Despite the declaration of sovereignty, Burawi exploration and settlement of the land was quite limited as a result of it's rugged terrain and attacks by indigenous peoples. [[Fort Samara]] (present-day [[Cusúsamara]]; {{Literal translation|New Samara}}) was founded in 15xx as the first permanent Burawi settlement in Balisca, operating as both a presidio and a [[W:Factory (trading post)|trading post]].  

==== Maroon conflict ====
{{Main|Maroon Wars|Maroon Confederacy|Maroon pirates}}
The land remained largely unexplored until the establishment of [[Daramazán]] 1617 by escaped Moro convicts and self-liberated Kaftian slaves. Due to its relatively brutal reputation, it was prohibitively known to colonists as [[Maroon Confederacy|'''Satanazes''']] ("isle of devils"), and it became a haven for [[Araucarian Passage|runaways,]] [[Maroon pirates|pirates]], and those [[Maroon Wars|actively fighting to interrupt slavery]]. Originally confined to the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Bayaran frontier]], this violent upheaval resulted in a substantial migration of people south into ''Pucará'', where it [[Maroon Wars|expanded exponentially]] and adopted [[W:Guerrilla warfare|guerrilla warfare]] to resist colonialist incursions.  

This diverse resistance gradually developed a society that adopted a range of {{W|Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous}} and [[Southwestern Kaftia|Southwestern Kaftian]] socio-political models, with aspects of [[W:Sephardic Jews|Sephardic]] and [[Mozbayán]] culture and specifically clan adaptations also included. It was decentralized in nature, operating as a {{W|confederation}} of nominally independent towns and settlements united by their resistance to Mesogean colonial encroachment.

[[Daramazán]] was founded as a colony 1617 by escaped Moro convicts and Kaftian slaves, at a time when the Araucaria Territory remained largely unexplored by Illypnians. As a refuge for those fleeing the colonial authorities, the territory became notorious for its gaudy displays of wealth and loose morals. It was a popular homeport for Mozarabic pirates from which to launch their raids on Spanish slave ships and settlements to liberate enslaved people, most of whom then joined the colony.  
[[File:Jaspar_Beckx_(tidl._tilskrevet)_-_Don_Miguel_de_Castro,_Emissary_of_Kongo_-_KMS7_-_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst.jpg|left|thumb|244x244px|[[Nsuukidem Jesumbo]], the Sanyanian ambassador to the [[Maroon Confederacy]] (1642)]]
[[File:Oath of the Ancestors painting.png|thumb|360x360px|[[Oath of the Ancestors|Juramento de los Ancestros]]  (1886) depicts a [[Maroon Wars|Maroon]] (left) and a [[W:Yoruba people|Imo]] woman (right) symbolizing the shared [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter|oath to resist against slavery]]]]
Oba [[Ekpedeme]] of [[Sanyan]] died in 1652, and was replaced by [[Nsuukidem of Sanyan|Nsuukidem]]. Nsuukidem, the son of former nobles, had himself served as an ambassador to the [[Maroon Confederacy]] and witnessed the cruelty of the {{W|chattel slavery}} that Sanyan was facilitating. He strongly denounced the practice, and threatened to end the arrangement. He was convinced otherwise by his advisors, who reminded him of the significant power imbalance between the foreign powers and Sanyan. In order the overcome this weakness, disrupt the slave trade, and quickly allow the prosecution of intercepted slavers and their vessels, Nsuukidem, supported by his council, and through his ties to figures within the [[Araucarian Passage]], financed the [[Maroon pirates]] to halt intercept and liberate Illypnian slave ships on their way to the [[Algarves]]. This was already a common practice of the Maroon pirates dating back to the founding of Daramazán, however as shown by historical records, pirate attacks intensified in the years after 1643.

This mutual alliance between the two sides was solidified by the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] of 1793 during the conflict, and the two came together to support the [[Asikyiran Revolution]].

The Ìlàlóye trade was significantly reduced with the [[Volisanian Civil War]], and even further with the beginning of the [[Baliscan War of Independence]]. The trade completely evaporated in 1808 with [[Kaftian Squadron]] enforcing the [[Kaftian anti-slave trade campaign]] from [[Joint Base Ajowa|Ajowa Naval Base]] in [[Sanyan]]. Western Kaftian slave markets, through which a majority of slaves sent to [[Lurando-Abyala]] had passed, largely collapsed following Volisania and Breisland halting the trade in 1815, and the outbreak of the [[Azawagh War]] was the ''[[W:Coup de grâce|coup de grâce]]'' for the slave trade. Oloye [[Kuyikabasi of Sanyan|Kuyikabasi]] officially abolished all forms of slavery that same year.

Following the victory of the Baliscan revolutionaries, the Maroon Confederacy supported Sanyan's efforts to return newly freed Kaftians to their places of origin through the island. The arrangement was a substantial failure on several fronts: the displaced Kaftians who sailed to the island were often unable to ever return to their original homes on the continent. Additionally, upon arrival in Sanyan, each recaptive was given a registration number, and information on their physical qualities would be entered into the [[Register of Liberated Kaftians]]. Oftentimes the documentation would be overwhelmingly subjective and would result in inaccurate entries, making them difficult to track. Further investigation revealed additional disparities in the entries of the re-captives, specifically in the names; many recaptives felt pressured to adopt [[Imo language|Imo]] or [[Kinza]] names to avoid potential maltreatment, which contributed greatly to the difficulty in locating them after they arrived. 

Between the outbreak of the first slave revolts in 1593, largely in [[Bayara]], and the [[Restaurasionistas|Volisan ''Restaurasionistas'']] attempt at reconquering the territory (the [[Baliscan War of Independence]]) in 1808, more than 375,000 formerly enslaved and free-born black people who faced social and legal oppression in the [[Aethiopic world]], had been liberated. Six Volisan naval expeditions invaded the territory in an attempt to conquer it between 1699 and 1736, but these were all beaten back with considerable loss of life as a result of violent guerrilla tactics and a lack of Volisan familiarity with the land. The Confederacy later intervened in the [[Baliscan Civil War]], playing a decisive role in beating back the [[Siege of Castejón]], and in subsequent years developed an amiable relationship with [[Anyos Federales|Reconstructionist Balisca]] with the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]]. The Confederacy continued to exercise sovereignty until it joined the [[Northern Confederation]] in [[Convention of Jatiel|1813]].

In [[Volisanya Boba|the period]] following the [[Volisanian Civil War|dissolution]] of the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]] and up until the opening days of the [[Baliscan War of Independence]], the Confederacy received limited diplomatic recognition from the [[Empire of Burawa]] and maintained extensive trade relations with the [[Hesperian League]]. It is through the merchant of the League that Maroons first learned of the civil war in Volisania.

=== Confederation with the Northern Confederation ===
{{Main|Northern Confederation|Convention of Jatiel}}
The Volisanian reconquest attempt of 1808 confronted the Maroon Confederacy with the strategic dilemma that had shadowed its existence since Nsuukidem's original calculation: the Confederacy could resist colonial power alone, but it could not defeat it alone. The opening 1809 campaign demonstrated that the Confederacy's military effectiveness depended on coordination with the [[Northern Confederation]], whose forces tied down Volisanian regiments in the north while Maroon guerrilla operations harassed the southern invasion columns. The two polities had fought as de facto allies throughout the War of Independence, sharing intelligence, coordinating operations, and sustaining each other's supply lines. The question after 1808 was whether this arrangement would remain ad hoc or become permanent.

The argument for formal confederation was led by the Confederacy's own military commanders, who had experienced the costs of operating without guaranteed northern support. The [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] had already created a web of obligations linking the Confederacy to [[Sanyan]] and, through Sanyan, to the Northern Confederation. The slave trade — the economic and moral foundation of the Confederacy's resistance economy — was collapsing. Piracy against slave ships had been the Confederacy's primary revenue source and raison d'être; with the trade evaporating, the Confederacy needed a new economic framework. The Northern Confederation offered access to infrastructure, trade networks, and reconstruction investment that the Confederacy could not generate independently. The argument against was led by the [[W:Griot|arokin assemblies]] — councils of elders and storytellers who served as the Confederacy's deliberative tradition — and by communities in the interior who feared that confederation would mean subordination to a distant northern authority no different in kind from the Volisanian colonial state they had spent two centuries fighting. The Griot assemblies argued that the Confederacy had survived six Volisanian invasions, the collapse of the slave trade, and two hundred years of colonial encirclement through independence, and that confederation would surrender the sovereignty that had made survival possible.

The debate was resolved by the [[Convention of Jatiel]], convened on 14 August 1813. The convention established the Maroon Confederacy as a [[W:Confederation#Confederated states|confederated republic]] of the Northern Confederation under the name ''Araucaria'' — officially adopting the geographic name already in use for the territory (after the araucaria pines abundant across its landscape). The Indigenous name ''Pucará'' was retained as a co-official designation. The convention guaranteed significant autonomy: internal self-governance through the existing arokin assembly structure, territorial integrity of the Confederacy's historic lands, independent militia organization under confederal coordination, and the right to maintain the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] relationship with Sanyan as a bilateral arrangement. In exchange, the Confederacy delegated foreign policy, [[W:Collective defense|collective defense]], and inter-republican trade to the confederal level. The convention was ratified by the Araucarian assemblies after the most contentious deliberation in the Confederacy's history — three communities in the interior refused ratification and maintained a posture of ''de facto'' independence for several years before gradually integrating.

The Convention of Jatiel transformed the Maroon Confederacy from a resistance state into a constituent polity within a larger confederation, without dissolving the internal structures that had sustained it. The Confederacy's military forces — the [[Maroon militias]] that had fought the Volisanian invasions — were reorganized as the [[Araucarian Territorial Defense Force]] under confederal command, though they retained their distinctive traditions, command structures, and the guerrilla doctrine that had made them formidable. The Confederacy's cooperative economic model, rooted in the [[W:Communal land|communal land]] tenure and collective labor systems of the Maroon settlements, became the foundation for Araucaria's later development as a {{W|participatory economics|participatory economy}}.

=== Baliscan Civil War and after ===
{{Main|Baliscan Civil War}}
[[File:SanPasqual.jpg|thumb|''Carga Araucariana'' (1884), depicting the [[Battle of Arrecife]]]]
The [[Baliscan Civil War]] (1828–1836) was the first major test of the confederal arrangement established by the Treaty of Axarquía. Araucarian forces, now fighting as part of the Northern Confederation rather than as an independent ally, played a decisive role in the conflict — most notably in the relief of the [[Siege of Castejón]], where Maroon militia units drawn from the communities that had debated confederation fifteen years earlier broke the siege lines alongside northern regiments. The war demonstrated that the confederal structure could withstand internal fracture and that Araucaria's integration had been genuine rather than nominal.

=== Early industrialization ===
{{Further|Industrialization in Balisca}}
Several men who aspired to become the new industrial magnates of this country would be men such as [[Tùmàs Lòkásàr]] (who founded the [[Araucarian Mining Company|Compañía Minera Araucariana]]), [[Alí-Buxàr Pùgmìjà]] (who pioneered industrialization of Araucaria's hemp production and refining by way of [[Hierbería|'''Hierbería Botica''']]), and [[Bàxkàl Kàmpàs]], who would found the [[Trans-Araucarian Railway Company|Compañía de Trenes Trans-Araucarianos]]. Amongst others, these individuals would play a crucial role in the industrialization of Araucaria, as well as its ability to enter into the status of an {{W|industrialized economy}} by the turn of the century. Araucaria's industrial growth was supported by the confederal government, which provided significant assistance with the development of the tools and technologies that would make the territory more suitable to the needs of an industrialized economy.
[[File:Imfezzan railroad workers, Jatiel.jpg|left|thumb|401x401px|[[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzan]] railroad workers at a post office in [[Jatiel]], during the construction of the [[Trans-Atlántido Railroad]]]]
Making use of its great wealth of {{W|natural resources}}, local Araucarian governments would contract out the extraction of its mineral riches to several foreign companies in exchange for a 40% stake in the mines and refineries that were established, as well as the employment of any local Araucarians in the regions where these resources were to be extracted. Strict contracts were written up to ensure that the imperial powers were excluded from making claims to any resources found within Araucaria — a task made easier by Araucaria's status as a confederal member since 1813, which placed it under the full protection of the confederal forces, alongside the obligations of the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]]. 

Unlike the [[periphery countries]] that had little knowledge of the legal practices of the imperial governments, Araucaria would additionally negotiate from a position of strength through its knowledge of {{W|Western law}} and the backing of the confederation.

The rapid influx of new migrants combined with Araucaria's high birth rate necessitated an accelerated {{W|industrialization}} process across the country, which allowed for and required an equally rapid development of the country's critical infrastructure, such as ports, factories, mills, railroads, and canals. Ports were quickly developed where the export of the natural resources of the country could be facilitated with ease, especially as the overall output of the interior mines increased with the rise of available workers. 

This in turn gave way to the construction of several {{W|steel mill}}s and arsenals that would be completed by the 1870s, and fuel the expansion of Araucaria's rail network. Both the cantonal administrations and the republican government led by [[Mùsà Màgròvèxò]] and his immediate successors would lean toward the funding of major public work initiatives, such as the [[Tenement Housing Act of 1870]] and the [[Trans-Matarrañyan Railroad]] in 1881, the latter of which would serve as the catalyst of Araucaria's deepening economic integration within the confederation.

=== Gilded Age and increasing radicalism ===
{{Main|Baliscan Gilded Age|Cantonal Revolution|Red Summer}}

Regardless, Araucaria's industrial capacity would continue at a high rate over the course of several years, encouraged by direct investment from both the republican government and local industrial magnates, as well as by the country's rapid population growth, which approached levels similar to that witnessed in settler colonies such as [[Potocsí]] and [[Balboa]]. Members of the [[Kaftian diaspora]] from across the globe, as well as [[Fosian Baliscans|Fosian]] and [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayani]] immigrants, would leave for Araucaria for the promise of a new life and a safe and secure future; Araucaria's population would double from about three million in 1850 to more than six million by 1890. By 1890, Araucaria had become one of the most industrialized areas in Balisca, as well as the most industrialized nations anywhere in [[Lurando-Abyala]].

The turn of the 20th century ushered in a period of immense prosperity for Araucaria. Fueled by the industrial boom, major cities like [[Axarquía]], Jatiel and [[Yerba Buena]] had emerged as bustling metropoli. Araucarian magnates, like Aleix Puigmitjà, the "Hemp Baron," and Pasqual Campas, the "Steel King," built vast fortunes on the back of resource extraction and infrastructure development. However, this age of prosperity wasn't without its shadows. The rapid industrialization led to harsh working conditions, particularly for the growing [[W:Proletariat|proletariat]]. [[Trade unions in Balisca|Labor unions]], usually affiliated with the [[Federación Anárquica del Trabajo]] and the [[Confederación Autónoma del Trabajo]], began to take root, advocating for better wages and safer working environments. Strikes and protests became commonplace, challenging the status quo.

The outbreak of [[W:World War I|World War I]] in 1914 had significant repercussions for Araucaria. While the Baliscan Confederation remained neutral, Araucaria’s economy experienced disruptions due to global trade interruptions. However, the demand for raw materials and manufactured goods from neutral nations allowed Araucaria to maintain economic stability.

===''Comuncazo'' and political dissolution===
{{Main|Red Summer|Tragic Week|Baliscan Revolution}}

However, this golden age was not without its shadows. The rapid, export-driven growth led to increasing societal stratification. The [[Volisan aristocracy]] in Cerdanya wielded disproportionate economic and political power at the confederal level, causing simmering unrest amongst the other republics. The {{W|First World War}} exacerbated these issues, disrupting trade and causing widespread economic turmoil.

The contentious [[1918 Cerdanyan general election]] saw the victory of Pol Pérez, a notorious crony capitalist. His election, widely condemned as fraudulent, sparked outrage across the confederation. Left-wing activists demanded the overthrow of the aristocracy and a re-run of the election, a call that quickly spread across the nation. On 28 October 1918, over four million students, laborers, and union members took to the streets in what would become known as the [[1918 Baliscan general strike]]. Major cities, including Araucaria’s vibrant coastal hubs, were brought to a standstill.

The movement gained confederal attention rapidly, especially after the assassination of key figures was linked to Pérez’s [[Progress Party (Cerdanya)|Progress Party]]. The government’s attempts to suppress the movement only fueled its growth. Protests intensified, with Concepción becoming a central hub of resistance. An anti-government gathering at the [[Universitat Cooperativa de Concepción]] ended in tragedy when the [[Carabineros de Balisca|''Carabineros'']] attacked, resulting in the deaths of 52 people. This incident galvanized protesters, who included liberals, libertarians, feminists, socialists, and anarchists, drawing international headlines.
[[File:Araucarian Council Republic.svg|thumb|300x300px|Flag of the [[Araucarian Socialist Council Republic]] (1918)]]
The movement escalated on 21 April 1919, with the [[storming of the Baliscan Cabildo]] by rebelling soldiers supported by civilians in reaction to increasingly violent repression by the state. The successful coup against the Confederal Congress and the seizure the Cabildo [[W:Power of the deed|instigated]] the second, more violent phase of the Baliscan Revolution, ultimately leading to a significant rise in {{W|political violence}} nationwide. Tensions further escalated with the assassination of Deputy Premier [[Moisès Vilchez]] by [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] [[Miquel Cebrián]]. With the country on the verge of a violent {{W|civil war}}, the Council of Ministers narrowly voted against {{W|vote of no confidence|confidence}} in Premier [[Máximo Burjassot]], forcing the administration to dissolve. Faced with continued unrest on the streets and an increasingly demoralized and hostile military, the Confederal Council called a [[1919 Confederal Convention|Confederal Convention]]. 

The political revolution that followed was transformative. It resulted in abolished the confederal republican system and implemented {{W|anarchism|anarchist}} and {{W|Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist}} principles. Internal borders were abolished, secularization was enacted, and {{W|Socialist self-management}} took root. Araucaria’s economy, like much of Balisca’s, was transformed with significant portions placed under {{W|worker control}}. Agrarian areas were collectivized and operated as libertarian socialist communes, while social welfare programs were expanded through income and {{W|wealth redistribution}}.

Socioeconomic policies were heavily influenced by the emerging [[W:Russian Futurism|Futurist]] movements, eventually coalescing into an [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] ideology. This period also accelerated the process of {{W|indigenous decolonization}} that had begun with the failed [[Cantonal Revolution]] and the subsequent [[1883 Confederal Convention]]. In Araucaria, the revolution brought about a renaissance of culture and social innovation. Worker committees took control of factories, and agrarian areas transformed into thriving, self-sustaining communes. The spirit of the revolution permeated every aspect of life, from art and literature to science and technology. Araucaria became a hub for progressive thought, attracting intellectuals and activists from around the world.
