===Politics===
{{Main|Politics of Araucaria}}

Exercising the {{W|devolution}} and {{W|autonomy}} granted to it in the Baliscan constitution, Araucaria maintains a {{W|Parliamentary system|parliamentary}} [[wikipedia:Constitutional republic|constitutional republic]], with a {{W|unicameralism|unicameral}} legislature, the [[Assembly of Araucaria]]. As stated in the Araucarian Constitution of 1952, all elections are {{W|Non-partisan democracy|non-partisan}}, meaning the Prime Minister is elected from the incoming membership of the Assembly, through multiple rounds of voting, called a {{W|contingent election}}. The Assembly is made up of 150 delegates who are directly elected by popular vote for two year terms utilizing {{W|single transferable vote|single transferable voting}}, and 15 seats reserved for [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|representatives of the Indigenous peoples of Araucaria]].

Prior to the [[Baliscan Revolution]], the [[W:Sovereigntism|sovereigntist]] [[New Araucarian League]] effectively maintained {{W|one-party dominant system}}. Following the Revolution, political parties experienced a rapid decline and eventual dissolution in the social upheaval of the [[Cosmocazo]]. In it's place, a vibrant {{W|non partisan democracy}} has emerged.

Support for [[Araucarian nationalism]] ranges from a demand for further autonomy and the [[W:Federalisation|federalisation]] of Balisca to the desire for independence from the rest of Balisca, expressed by Araucarian separatists. Support for independence tends to be higher amongst [[Kafto-Baliscans]] and [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayanis]], and is often tied to {{W|Black separatism|Black}} and [[Mozbayana|Mozbayán]]-separatist sentiment. In a survey published by [[El País]] in 2010, 32% of respondents expressed support for Araucarian independence if a referendum was to be held the next day. In February of the same year, a poll by the [[Cooperative University of Vitacura]] gave more or less the same results. Other polls have shown lower support for independence, ranging from 18 to 36%. Although it is established in the whole of the territory, support for independence is significantly higher in the [[Rural society in Balisca|hinterland]], away from the more populated coastal areas like [[Axarquía]].

==Economy==
