=== Climate ===
The community’s territory, on the windward side of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] is significantly moderated by the [[Aethiopian Ocean]]. Biomes present in Araucaria range from {{W|temperate rain forests}} ([[W:Araucaria moist forests|Araucarian moist forests]]) in the north, dry inland forests and semi-arid valleys to the range and canyon districts of the Central Interior, to boreal forest and subarctic prairie in the Northern Interior. High mountainous regions stretching from north to south have {{W|subalpine flora}} and {{W|subalpine climate}}. Rain forest vegetation is concentrated primarily on the western coast of Araucaria, as a result of the influence of the {{W|Brazil Current}}.

The climate of Araucaria is diverse due to its varied topography and the influence of the surrounding bodies of water. Northeastern Araucaria has a [[W:Warm-summer Mediterranean climate|warm-summer Mediterranean climate]] with warm temperatures and wet winters, autumns, and springs, and relatively dry summers. This area benefits from the moderating effects of the [[Aethiopian Ocean]], which help to keep temperatures mild and precipitation regular. Some elevated locations in the southeast (Vitacura) have the rare cold-summer Mediterranean climate, characterized by cooler summers and snowy winters.

The western coast of Araucaria, particularly the peninsula of Cabo Traïdor, experiences a temperate rain forest climate. This area receives heavy rainfall throughout the year, primarily due to the moist air brought by the Brazil Current. The rain falls mainly in autumn and winter, with July being the rainiest month (107 mm). The average annual rainfall totals 480 mm a year in the far west, but precipitation decreases as one travels further east. The abundant rainfall supports lush, dense forests with a rich biodiversity.

The central region of Araucaria has a much more varied climate, ranging from {{W|semi-arid}} valleys and dry inland forests to {{W|subarctic prairie}}s and {{W|boreal forests}}. These regions experience greater temperature extremes, with hot summers and cold winters. This area is home to the The high mountain areas have a subalpine climate, characterized by cool summers and cold, snowy winters. These areas are home to unique flora and fauna adapted to the harsher conditions. The eastern Matarrañya region is notable for its numerous volcanoes, a few of which reach altitudes significantly higher than the rest of the mountains. This volcanic activity contributes to the region’s diverse landscapes and geothermal features, attracting tourists and researchers alike.

In the Jatiel area, the average high temperatures are about {{Convert|30|C|F}} in the summer months of January and February and {{Convert|15|C|F}} in the winter months of June and July. The average monthly precipitation is no more than a trace in January and February and 69.7 millimeters (2.74 in) in June and July. By contrast, in Axarquía the average high temperatures are somewhat lower in the summer at {{Convert|23|C|F}} but higher in the winter at 13 °C (55 °F), and the amount of rain is much greater. In the summer, [[Almocatén]] receives an average of twenty millimeters of rain per month; in June and July, the city is pounded by an average of {{Convert|253|mm|}} per month. The numerous rivers greatly increase their flow as a result of the winter rains and the spring melting of the Matarrañya snows, and they contract considerably in the summer.

=== Flora and Fauna ===
[[File:Jaguar.jpg|left|thumb|A black {{W|jaguar}} (''panthera onca''); such {{W|Melanism|melanistic}} jaguars are commonly called {{W|black panthers}}. They are the [[W:National mammal|national mammal]] of Araucaria]] 
The natural vegetation of Araucaria is mainly the [[W:Valdivian temperate rainforests|Araucarian temperate rainforests]] along with the {{W|Antarctic Floristic Kingdom}}. These forests are characterized by large trees, chiefly evergreen ''[[W:Nothofagus|Nothofagus]]'' and [[W:Conifer|Conifers]] plus [[W:Myrtaceae|Myrtles]]. 

The [[W:Understory|understory]] is made of [[W:Vine|vines]], [[W:Hanging vine|hanging vines]], bushes, small trees, [[W:Moss|moss]], dead [[W:Trunk (botany)|trunks]] and decomposing matter. Despite being largely [[W:Evergreen|evergreen]] the Valdivian temperate rainforests do contain a number of [[W:Deciduous|deciduous]] tree species like ''[[W:Nothofagus obliqua|Nothofagus obliqua]]'' and ''[[W:Nothofagus alpina|Nothofagus alpina]]''. Other vegetations types of southern Araucaria include ''[[Fitzroya]]'' forests, ''[[W:Araucaria araucana|Araucaria]]'' forests and wetlands called [[W:Ñadis|ñadis]].

